rticle-title>Towards a better understanding of memory-based reasoning systems.</article-title><source>International Conference on Machine Learning</source><fpage>242</fpage><lpage>250</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig id="pone-0000337-g001" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000337.g001</object-id><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>The ROC curve of recall experiment by 5-fold cross validation. Sensitivity is defined as #TP/(#TP&#x0002b;#FN), and specificity is defined as #TN/(#FP&#x0002b;#TN).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000337.g001"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000337-g002" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000337.g002</object-id><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>#TP at 50&#x00025; precision (Upper) and #TP at 80&#x00025; precision (Lower). Here, <italic>ppi</italic>, <italic>exp</italic>, <italic>motif</italic>, <italic>pheno</italic>, and <italic>locali</italic> corresponds to PPI, gene expression, protein motif, phenotype, and localization data, respectively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000337.g002"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000337-g003" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000337.g003</object-id><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>#TP at 50&#x00025; precision (Left) and #TP at 80&#x00025; precision (Right) with varying amount of PPI edges. At 50&#x00025; precision, the integration model always wins over the PPI model. However, at 80&#x00025; precision, the integration model wins only when more than 50&#x00025; of original PPI edges are present.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000337.g003"/></fig></sec><fn-group><fn fn-type="conflict"><p><bold>Competing Interests: </bold>The authors have declared that no competing interests exist.</p></fn><fn fn-type="financial-disclosure"><p><bold>Funding: </bold>This work was supported by NSF grant ITR-048715 and NHGRI grant R01 HG003367-01A1.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>